Comments: [Evaluator: Christian Iliadis; Date: 08/27/2024]
1.  Resonance energies from Ex in Endt 1998 and nuclear Q value.
2.  Er >= 601 keV: wg from Endt 1990, except for Er = 601, 624 keV for which the strengths of Moazen et al. 2011 were adopted. 
3.  Er = 374 keV: partial widths from measured (p,a), (p,g), (a,g) strengths.
4.  Er = 601, 624 keV: partial widths are derived from measured wg_pg, wg_pa, wg_ag.
5.  Er = 393 keV: proton width from (3He,d) C2S value; upper limit on Ga is found from measured branching ratios (Ross et al. 1995) and assuming Gg = 1 eV.
6.  Er = 197 keV: the level has Jp = (1,2)-; 1- gives better agreement (factor 2) between transfer Gp and Gp calculated from wg_pg; 2+ gives agreement within
    factor 4; we will adopt Jp = 1-. The assumption Gg = 1 eV is an approximate estimate; Ga is estimated from experimental upper limit on wg_pa in Iliadis et al. 
    1993.
7.  Er = 203 keV: level is not populated in (3He,d); assume C2S_p = 0.01, which yields Gp < 1.1e-11 eV (ell_p = 4). Ga is estimated from C2S_a = 0.1. There is
    intensity observed at the location of this level in the published (6Li,d) and (7Li,t) spectra [Tanabe et al. 1981; Madhusoodhanan et al. 1999], but no C2S 
    values were reported.
8.  Er = 161 keV: partial widths are estimated from measured wg_ag, C2S from (3He,d), and branching ratios reported in Ross et al. 1995.
9.  Er = -1.4 keV: reduced width from (3He,d); Gg and Ga from Aboud et al. 2018.
10. Er = 1470 keV (Ex = 10333 keV, Jp = 1-): Gp = 2.3 keV and Ga = 3.8 keV from Fang, PhD thesis (TUNL, 1987).
